{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvrBLiSQzEnzesa4JJUK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FctzDHjPfGnS89Fade2W3e","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(old,new,cnt=1):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==cnt,(s.count(old),old[:60])\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\n\nrep(r\"\\title{Regression-Based Global Effect Estimation Under Network Interference: Exposure-Mapping Fidelity Governs Accuracy}\",\n    r\"\\title{How Accurate Is Regression-Based Global Effect Estimation Under Network Interference? An Eight-Case Synthetic Evaluation}\")\n\n# abstract\na=s.index(r\"\\begin{abstract}\")+len(r\"\\begin{abstract}\")\nb=s.index(r\"\\end{abstract}\")\ns=s[:a]+r\"\"\"\nIn online experiments on social networks and marketplaces, a user's outcome depends on other users' treatments, and the quantity of interest is the global average treatment effect (GATE): the mean outcome difference between treating everyone and treating no one. Regression-based estimators fit an outcome model on exposure features from one randomized experiment and difference its predictions at the all-treated and all-control corners, but how accurate they are, and what determines their error, is poorly characterized. We measure this on an eight-case synthetic grid (3000 nodes; stochastic-block, Barab\\'asi--Albert and Watts--Strogatz graphs; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), scored on 24 unseen seeds. A frozen estimator that selects among 15 low-parameter exposure mappings with a BIC gate reaches a mean relative RMSE of 0.126, against 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression, 0.622 for difference-in-means, 0.994 for a neighbourhood Horvitz--Thompson estimator, and 0.045 for an oracle regression given the true mapping. Per-case error is 0.031--0.054 in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction, across three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs, and 0.151--0.452 in the saturating, count-by-degree and two-hop cases. We hypothesize that exposure-mapping misspecification and corner extrapolation drive the larger errors, but the grid is not factorial and the estimator's per-case mapping choices were not verified, so this account is untested. Nominal 95\\% intervals cover only 0.839 of the time. The evidence is limited to one graph size, case specifications shared between model selection and final scoring, and single-factor contrasts without per-case intervals.\n\"\"\"+s[b:]\n\n# intro thesis\nrep(r\"\"\"Our reading of the evidence is that \\emph{accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism} (a concern the interference literature raises under the heading of misspecified exposure mappings and interference of unknown form; \\citealp{Savje2023Misspecified,Savje2021Unknown}), not by graph family, interference strength or cluster-versus-Bernoulli assignment within the tested range, and that the model-based intervals are miscalibrated regardless.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"The measured pattern is that per-case error is low in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction and higher in the three cases whose form is not, with no visible degradation across graph family, interference strength (up to three times larger) or cluster-versus-Bernoulli assignment within the tested range, and that the model-based intervals undercover. A natural hypothesis, which the interference literature raises under the heading of misspecified exposure mappings and interference of unknown form \\citep{Savje2023Misspecified,Savje2021Unknown}, is that accuracy depends on how well the fitted exposure mapping matches the generating mechanism. We state this as a hypothesis: the grid is not factorial, and the mapping the estimator selected in each case was not recorded in verified form.\"\"\")\n\nrep(r\"with single-factor case contrasts that point to exposure-mapping misspecification as the dominant error source. (iii)\",\n    r\"with single-factor case contrasts that are consistent with, but do not establish, exposure-mapping misspecification as the main error source. (iii)\")\nrep(r\"and two other estimator classes (a flexible ridge ensemble and a linear model with a design-weighted residual correction) did not beat plain linear regression on the model-selection seeds.\",\n    r\"and two other estimator classes (a flexible ridge ensemble and a linear model with a design-weighted residual correction) scored worse than the gated library on the model-selection seeds.\")\nrep(r\"\"\"\\n\\n\\\\paragraph{Designs for the GATE.}\"\"\", \"\\n\\n\"+r\"\\paragraph{Designs for the GATE.}\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n 'sec:corner' paper.tex | head -3"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2129,"cache_read_input_tokens":121876,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2129},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9055c2ad-8868-47aa-af47-ddab8ec71d32","uuid":"211709c6-fb13-483c-914c-46d67b83bb03","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:46:28.362Z","request_id":"req_011CgvrBKvaBf3t38zSW1wMN"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FctzDHjPfGnS89Fade2W3e","type":"tool_result","content":"61:\\paragraph{Misspecified exposure mappings.} \\citet{Savje2023Misspecified} treats a misspecified analyst mapping and separates the estimand's definition from the assumptions that make an estimator informative about it; \\citet{Savje2021Unknown} study average-effect estimation under interference of unknown form. These works are analytical; we instead measure in a simulation grid how far a regression GATE estimator lands from the all-treated-minus-all-control contrast when its mapping does or does not match the mechanism, and offer the corner-extrapolation account of Section~\\ref{sec:corner} as a heuristic.\n172:\\subsection{A heuristic account of the corner extrapolation}\\label{sec:corner}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9055c2ad-8868-47aa-af47-ddab8ec71d32","uuid":"6b300500-c42a-412e-8695-92c45e781a43","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:46:28.440Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"61:\\paragraph{Misspecified exposure mappings.} \\citet{Savje2023Misspecified} treats a misspecified analyst mapping and separates the estimand's definition from the assumptions that make an estimator informative about it; \\citet{Savje2021Unknown} study average-effect estimation under interference of unknown form. These works are analytical; we instead measure in a simulation grid how far a regression GATE estimator lands from the all-treated-minus-all-control contrast when its mapping does or does not match the mechanism, and offer the corner-extrapolation account of Section~\\ref{sec:corner} as a heuristic.\n172:\\subsection{A heuristic account of the corner extrapolation}\\label{sec:corner}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
