{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvrDEAbWXhdFeSQBbeC4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2138,"cache_read_input_tokens":126127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2138},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9055c2ad-8868-47aa-af47-ddab8ec71d32","uuid":"b18ccb1d-db09-4ba1-bf73-82aa6c39ede6","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:46:45.416Z","request_id":"req_011CgvrDDJWBmcmaJssC5Eez"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvrDEAbWXhdFeSQBbeC4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012Ww9s7hkKJgYqYmmTAhAij","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(old,new,cnt=1):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==cnt,(s.count(old),old[:70])\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\n\nrep(r\"\\subsection{Where the error concentrates: exposure-mapping fidelity}\", r\"\\subsection{Where the error concentrates: per-case contrasts}\")\nrep(r\"Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b and Table~\\ref{tab:cases} show that per-case error is bimodal. In the five cases whose generating form is a linear function of the one-hop neighbour fraction (cases 1--5), the frozen estimator's relative RMSE lies between 0.031 and 0.054.\",\n    r\"Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b and Table~\\ref{tab:cases} show that per-case error is bimodal across the eight cases. In the five cases whose generating form is a linear function of the one-hop neighbour fraction (cases 1--5), the frozen estimator's relative RMSE lies between 0.031 and 0.054.\")\nrep(r\"\"\"These contrasts are consistent with the thesis that misspecification of the exposure mapping, not graph family, strength or assignment, is what moves the error, and they are inconsistent with a simple story in which heavier-tailed degree distributions or stronger spillovers alone degrade regression GATE estimation in this range. They do not prove it: the grid is not factorial, the cases that fail are also the three that depart from the linear fraction form, and each pair changes more than one property at a time in at least two instances. The oracle's plane mean of 0.045 is similar to the per-case errors in cases 1--5, suggesting residual sampling noise at $n=3000$, though this compares a plane mean with per-case values.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"These contrasts are consistent with the hypothesis that the mismatch between the generating form and a fraction-linear mapping, rather than graph family, strength or assignment, is what moves the error, and they do not support a simple story in which heavier-tailed degree distributions or stronger spillovers alone degrade regression GATE estimation in this range. They do not establish it: the grid is not factorial, the three cases with higher error are also the three that depart from the linear fraction form, each pair changes more than one property at a time in at least two instances, and we did not record which mapping the estimator selected, so a mapping mismatch is not shown for any case. Case 6 is a caution: the library contains its exact generating sigmoid, yet the error is 0.198, so a missing mapping alone cannot explain it; failure of the gate to select that mapping, averaging over several mappings, or finite-sample variance at the corners are all possible and untested. The oracle's plane mean of 0.045 is similar to the per-case errors in cases 1--5, suggesting residual sampling noise at $n=3000$, though this compares a plane mean with per-case values.\"\"\")\n\nrep(r\"The pattern in Table~\\ref{tab:cases} fits a simple account that we offer as interpretation, not as a tested mechanism.\",\n    r\"The pattern in Table~\\ref{tab:cases} can be read through a simple account that we offer as interpretation, not as a tested mechanism.\")\nrep(r\"If the generating term is nonlinear\", r\"If the generating term is nonlinear\",0) if False else None\nrep(r\"For the sigmoid in case 6 (centre 0.5, steepness 10) the slope is largest at the middle, exactly where the data lie, so a linear fit extrapolates a steep local slope to the corners.\",\n    r\"For the sigmoid in case 6 (centre 0.5, steepness 10) the slope is largest at the middle, exactly where the data lie, so a linear fit would extrapolate a steep local slope to the corners; whether the gated estimator used a sigmoid or fell back to linear in this case is not known.\")\n\n# bias/variance section\na=s.index(r\"\\subsection{Bias, variance and the gated library}\")\nb=s.index(r\"\\subsection{Reliability: seed variability and interval coverage}\")\nnew=r\"\"\"\\subsection{Bias, variance and the model-selection comparison}\nThe mean absolute relative bias on the final plane is 0.074, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.bias_rel_abs_mean]]\n which is 0.588 of the mean relative RMSE. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#bias_over_rmse]]\n Per-case bias is not recorded, so a substantial systematic component exists on average but is not shown to be concentrated in the cases with higher error.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Model-selection plane (8 seeds 101--108, same eight case specifications, different seeds): plane-mean relative RMSE for the three estimator classes, each scored once. These are selection-set numbers, not final evidence; per-case values, baselines, bias and coverage for this plane are not used here.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n\\toprule\nEstimator class & Plane-mean rel.\\ RMSE\\\\\n\\midrule\nFlexible ridge ensemble & 0.232\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_001]]\nLinear + design-weighted residual correction & 0.185\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_002]]\nBIC-gated exposure library (selected) & 0.140\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_003]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three estimator classes on the eight model-selection seeds. The gated library has the lowest plane-mean error (0.140), against 0.185 for the linear model with a design-weighted, shrunk corner-residual correction and 0.232 for a flexible ridge ensemble with extrapolation-aware averaging. Because the evaluator's per-case and baseline values for this plane are not part of the verified evidence we use, we draw no per-case or baseline-relative conclusion from it, and in particular we do not say here whether the two alternatives beat plain linear regression. The three classes differ in several respects at once (model family, selection rule, interval construction), so this comparison supports a ranking of the three configurations on these seeds and no attribution to any single component. The library did contain a two-hop walk-fraction mapping, and the final-plane error in the two-hop case remains 0.452; why it did not resolve case 8 is \\emph{unknown}. The two-hop feature in the source counts length-two walks, including walks that return to the node, and so differs from the generating distance-exactly-two fraction; whether this matters, whether the gate ever selected it, and whether the all-treated corner is poorly constrained were not tested. No ablation of the gate margin or library components was run.\n\n\"\"\"\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\n\n# validity\nrep(r\"\"\"\n close to DiM's 0.622: once the exposure features are scrambled, regression recovers only the direct effect, which indicates that the estimator is using the real graph. No control was vacuous,\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"\n which is similar to the difference-in-means value of 0.622 and much larger than the 0.126 headline. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\n We do not interpret the similarity mechanistically. No control was vacuous,\"\"\")\nrep(r\"so the estimator does not create effects from nothing.\", r\"so the estimator returned an essentially zero estimate when the outcome carried no signal.\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n \"plane_consistency\\|fig:planes\\|Figure~\\\\\\\\ref{fig:planes}\" paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2138,"cache_read_input_tokens":126127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2138},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9055c2ad-8868-47aa-af47-ddab8ec71d32","uuid":"f038faf8-7d00-4a39-b44e-17e9f8960294","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:46:59.824Z","request_id":"req_011CgvrDDJWBmcmaJssC5Eez"}
