{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvrEXxh7nqe93kqhdYos","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2893,"cache_read_input_tokens":128265,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2893},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9055c2ad-8868-47aa-af47-ddab8ec71d32","uuid":"0e6420d0-5b3c-465c-a10b-e4313ddf9fb6","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:47:04.245Z","request_id":"req_011CgvrEX6cV8QCujymKdvRn"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvrEXxh7nqe93kqhdYos","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DPvpLW1zemjGnjKak2PsJ2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(old,new,cnt=1):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==cnt,(s.count(old),old[:70])\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\n\nrep(r\"it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling.\",\n    r\"it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, treats units as independent in the sandwich estimate even under the cluster design, and uses no resampling (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py}, \\texttt{sandwich\\_se}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}). Cluster-robust and network-aware variance estimation is a developed topic \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Han2023ModelbasedRA}; the interval here is not of that kind.\")\nrep(r\" Variance estimators for design-based effects \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC}, block-bootstrap intervals for regression adjustment \\citep{Han2023ModelbasedRA} and random-graph asymptotics \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph} address network dependence; we did not run them on this grid, so we cannot say whether they would restore nominal coverage.\",\n    r\" Variance estimators for design-based effects, block-bootstrap intervals for regression adjustment and random-graph asymptotics \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph} address network dependence; we did not run any of them on this grid, so we cannot say whether they would restore nominal coverage.\")\n\n# discussion\nrep(r\"On this grid, regression GATE estimation with a correctly shaped exposure mapping is accurate (3--5\\% relative RMSE per case at $n=3000$) across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. When the mapping is misspecified, per-case error rises from 0.031--0.054 to 0.151--0.452. Because these are the tested regime, we do not extrapolate to other sizes, densities or mechanisms. Practically, the results suggest spending effort on the exposure mapping, for instance by model comparison among a small family. A gate of this kind has costs: in the model-selection comparison, the library improved the saturating case but was worse than the linear-plus-correction estimator on the count$\\times$degree case, so a library is not uniformly better than a simpler fit, and the choice of margin and members is itself a source of researcher degrees of freedom that we did not ablate.\",\n    r\"On this grid, the frozen regression GATE estimator is accurate (relative RMSE 0.031--0.054 per case at $n=3000$) in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour fraction, across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. In the saturating, count$\\times$degree and two-hop cases the per-case error is 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452. Whether this reflects exposure-mapping misspecification, as the literature on misspecified mappings would suggest, is a hypothesis: we did not verify which mapping was selected in any case, and case 6 shows that the library's containing the true form does not guarantee a low error. Because these are the tested regime, we do not extrapolate to other sizes, densities or mechanisms. A tentative practical reading is that effort spent on the exposure mapping, for instance by model comparison among a small family, is worth testing; this study does not isolate it. A gate of this kind also has costs we did not ablate: the choice of margin and library members is a source of researcher degrees of freedom.\")\nrep(r\"The evaluator produces mechanisms whose forms the library partly anticipates (the library includes sigmoids centred at 0.5 and saturating and count-based shapes), so the gain over linear regression reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression.\",\n    r\"The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression.\")\nrep(r\"(5) The cause of the two-hop failure and of the undercoverage is not established. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds.\",\n    r\"(5) The cause of the larger errors in cases 6--8 (including the two-hop failure) and of the undercoverage is not established, and the mapping selected per case was not verified, so the mapping-mismatch account is a hypothesis. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds, and their per-case results are not used.\")\n\n# conclusion\na=s.index(r\"\\section{Conclusion}\")\nb=s.index(\"% IMPORTANT: \\\\clearpage\")\ns=s[:a]+r\"\"\"\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured how well a regression-based GATE estimator recovers the truth under network interference on an eight-case synthetic grid. Relative RMSE was 0.031--0.054 per case in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction, regardless of graph family, a threefold increase in interference strength, or cluster versus Bernoulli assignment, and 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452 in the saturating, count-by-degree and two-hop cases. A BIC-gated library of parametric mappings lowered mean relative RMSE to 0.126 from 0.180 for plain linear regression, but remained 2.8 times above an oracle given the true mapping, did not fix the two-hop case, and produced intervals that cover only 0.839 of the time. We hypothesize that exposure-mapping mismatch and extrapolation to the all-treated and all-control corners drive the larger errors; this account is consistent with the single-factor contrasts but untested, because the grid is not factorial and the selected mappings were not verified. These findings are limited to one size, one treatment probability, case pairs without per-case intervals, and shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring. The most direct next steps are a factorial extension over size, density and treatment probability with per-case intervals, an ablation of the gate and library with logged mapping choices, diagnostics for the two-hop failure, calibrated intervals (for example, cluster-aware resampling), and experiments on public graphs.\n\n\"\"\"+s[b:]\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2893,"cache_read_input_tokens":128265,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2893},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9055c2ad-8868-47aa-af47-ddab8ec71d32","uuid":"aa79d8c3-501c-44af-a27b-5d29736a87c6","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:47:16.033Z","request_id":"req_011CgvrEX6cV8QCujymKdvRn"}
