{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WRzc1MtuA6VLqGCiwTkjpK","type":"tool_result","content":"20:\\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{2}{Overview. A randomized assignment on a network yields exposure features (own treatment, neighbour-treated fraction or count, optional two-hop and degree terms); a fitted outcome regression is evaluated at the observed assignment and at the all-treated and all-control corners, and the average difference is the GATE estimate. The eight-case grid varies graph family, response shape, spillover range, strength and assignment design. Error can come from sampling noise or from misspecification of the exposure mapping, which matters most at the rarely observed corners}{figure.caption.4}{}}\n56:\\newlabel{fig:main}{{2}{5}{Final plane, 24 unseen seeds. (a) Mean relative RMSE over the eight cases for the oracle, the frozen estimator, linear exposure regression, difference-in-means (DiM) and neighbourhood HT (log axis). (b) Frozen-estimator relative RMSE per case (SBM: Bernoulli assignment; clus.: SBM with cluster assignment; str.: strong interference; sat.: saturating; c$\\times $d: count$\\times $degree; 2-hop: two-hop); dashed lines are the oracle and linear plane means. Per-case values for the reference estimators are not available}{figure.caption.15}{}}\n73:\\newlabel{fig:rel}{{3}{8}{(a) Per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases for the 24 final seeds, with the mean and a 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval for the mean (10{,}000 resamples over seeds). (b) Mean 95\\%-interval coverage over cases against the nominal level, with mean relative interval width}{figure.caption.18}{}}\n79:\\newlabel{sec:limits}{{5}{9}{Discussion and Limitations}{section.5}{}}\n85:\\newlabel{endbody}{{6}{10}{Conclusion}{section.6}{}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9055c2ad-8868-47aa-af47-ddab8ec71d32","uuid":"da94481d-8a5e-45b1-aa1a-163dae23dc99","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:48:10.369Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"20:\\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{2}{Overview. A randomized assignment on a network yields exposure features (own treatment, neighbour-treated fraction or count, optional two-hop and degree terms); a fitted outcome regression is evaluated at the observed assignment and at the all-treated and all-control corners, and the average difference is the GATE estimate. The eight-case grid varies graph family, response shape, spillover range, strength and assignment design. Error can come from sampling noise or from misspecification of the exposure mapping, which matters most at the rarely observed corners}{figure.caption.4}{}}\n56:\\newlabel{fig:main}{{2}{5}{Final plane, 24 unseen seeds. (a) Mean relative RMSE over the eight cases for the oracle, the frozen estimator, linear exposure regression, difference-in-means (DiM) and neighbourhood HT (log axis). (b) Frozen-estimator relative RMSE per case (SBM: Bernoulli assignment; clus.: SBM with cluster assignment; str.: strong interference; sat.: saturating; c$\\times $d: count$\\times $degree; 2-hop: two-hop); dashed lines are the oracle and linear plane means. Per-case values for the reference estimators are not available}{figure.caption.15}{}}\n73:\\newlabel{fig:rel}{{3}{8}{(a) Per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases for the 24 final seeds, with the mean and a 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval for the mean (10{,}000 resamples over seeds). (b) Mean 95\\%-interval coverage over cases against the nominal level, with mean relative interval width}{figure.caption.18}{}}\n79:\\newlabel{sec:limits}{{5}{9}{Discussion and Limitations}{section.5}{}}\n85:\\newlabel{endbody}{{6}{10}{Conclusion}{section.6}{}}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
